====================================================================================================
LIMITS THE WRITE-UP MUST STATE (spec section 13)
====================================================================================================
  1. Matched muons have a PF partner; LJ muons by definition should not. The matching failure selects on track quality, so the HYPOTHESIS was T_matched <= T_LJ, making Pred a LOWER BOUND on feed-up. MEASURED HERE, THAT DIRECTION IS CONTRADICTED: the spec-section-8 test finds Pred_SB > Obs_SB in a number of slices (see the 'hard transport failure' table), and a lower bound cannot exceed the observation. Limit 1 must be stated as a refuted hypothesis, not as a fact; the sideband non-closure in a slice is the floor on any tail-closure claim in that slice. Slicing reduces but cannot remove the population mismatch; a joint |eta| x nHits matrix is not booked.
  2. The two populations are NOT disjoint in code: the LJ cross-cleaning is a SEGMENT match, the control a dR < 0.2 match, so a dR-matched but segment-unmatched DSA muon sits in both M and L/Obs. The overlap is unmeasurable with the booked histograms; it biases closure toward 1 and under-states errors. A segment-match flag (or a dR axis) on the matched matrices is the recommended addition for the next rerun.
  3. The fold explains the LJ tail from the matched-sample rates; it does not explain why data feeds up ~3.5x more than MC -- that is a detector question.
  4. Stage 1 addresses only feed-up from below the source cut (~26 % of the matched sample's own tail); the residual BOUNDS, never measures, a genuine high-pT component.
  5. Circularity is corrected within the source block and bracketed by sigma_circ, not removed.  MEASURED HERE: the naive fold's bias does NOT have the universal high sign the specification assumed, and the EM recursion has not converged at the pre-registered iteration 3 (see the METHOD-CLAIM checks); the remaining distance to its fixed point is folded into sigma_circ, which can only lower R_cons.
  6. Per-slice MC closure is a +-30-50 % statement on ~10-20 effective events and confounds transport with genuine content. All 8/8 MC samples are present, so this bias is retired; the statement remains that the test rests on ~10-20 effective events.
  7. No bulk-normalised rate may be compared across data and MC with the 8/8 tail-selected set (R4); G_up is a declared ~1.8x over-estimate for that reason.
  8. The [1.2,2.4) |eta| slice averages a factor-~4 internal spread in the data/MC transfer ratio (peak near |eta| 1.6-1.8, trough near 2.2-2.4); kappa_pop is the only handle.
  9. Wrong dR partners inflate T at the 1e-3 level where the low-row tail probabilities live; the relPtErr table exposes but cannot bound it (a 0.2 < dR < 0.4 control would).
  10. Cosmic templates are data-only and selection-biased (R8: the vertex-inverted template is ~94 % high-nHits and selects on cosAlpha, which a short track reconstructs worst); the phi test bounds only a VERTICAL component.
  11. CR and VR share most events; the |eta| and nHits slicings share the same muons; no quadrature combination of their pulls is legitimate. Two DSA muons of one LJ are correlated; the errors assume independence.
  12. Data are 68/68 chunks. Closures need no luminosity, but T14 and the era table depend on an external era-lumi table until 68/68 land; the verdict is PROVISIONAL.
  13. The thresholds 3 sigma / 1/3 / 2 sigma / 0.25 / 0.10 are PRE-REGISTERED CHOICES, not derived numbers. 'Consistent with feed-up at factor-1.5 precision' remains the strongest honest formulation unless R_cons and A_low are flat across the scan.

